==English==

===Etymology===
{{prefix|in|existence|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# The state of not [[being]], not [[existing]], or not being [[perceptible]].
#* {{quote-book|1648|[[w:Robert Boyle|Robert Boyle]]|Seraphic Love|edition=1997 Kessinger ed.|isbn=1564590089|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=T2MXamBFMOYC|page=57
|passage=Our '''inexistence''' indeed was a condition, wherein nothing in us was capable of being a motive of God's love; but our enmity proceeded further, and made us worthy of his detestation; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|1941|Giuseppe di Gioia|Swift are the Shadows|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=yoodAAAAMAAJ|page=78
|passage=In order to prove the '''inexistence''' of God, he challenged Him to strike him down in five minutes while timing himself with a watch.}}
#* {{quote-book|2007|Jacques-Alain Miller|chapter=The Sinthome, A Mixture of Symptom and Fantasy|The Later Lacan|isbn=0791469972|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=89TBHTALjdgC|page=57
|passage=Axiomatics (namely, that everything that will be used for the purposes of a demonstration is explained) does nothing more than formalizing this wiping clean — in other words, '''inexistence''' is posed as the condition for necessity to emerge.}}
# The state of existing in something
#* {{quote-book|1663|[[w:Isaac Barrow|Isaac Barrow]]|chapter=A Defence of the Blessed Trinity|The Theological Works of Isaac Barrow|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=T38NAAAAQAAJ|page=188|year_published=1830
|passage=that there is a mutual '''inexistence''' of one in all, and all in one; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|1854|Christopher Walton|Notes and Materials for an Adequate Biography of the Celebrated Divine and Theosopher: William Law|url=http://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015019746513|page=207
|passage=She distinguished as to this, the '''inexistence''' in God from eternity, and the figurative manifestation in time.}}
#* {{quote-book|2005|Louis Dupre|The Enlightenment and the Intellectual Foundations of Modern Culture|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=UeiNVFNKgjcC|isbn=0300113463|page=303
|passage=Berkeley's theory of the creature's permanent '''inexistence''' in God evoked a suspicion of pantheism.}}
# That which exists within; a [[constituent]].
#: {{rfquotek|A. Tucker}}

====Usage notes====
* In modern philosophical writing, this is chiefly used with the sense "nonexistence" as a literal translation or [[calque]] of a corresponding term in another European language, such as the German {{term|lang=de|Inexistenz}} or the Spanish {{term|lang=es|inexistencia}}.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|not existing}} [[nonexistence]], [[absence]], [[lack]]
* {{sense|existing within}} [[inherence]]

